<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="integrations-okta"
      updated="August 25, 2026"
      evidence="Live-tested against an Okta Integrator org on August 25, 2026: OIDC sign-in, user lifecycle, group push and rename, group-based role and runner access, and delete/recreate isolation passed."
      toc={[
        {"sso", "Single sign-on"},
        {"scim", "Directory sync"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="integrations-okta">
        <:dek>
          Connect Okta to emisar: one OIDC web app for sign-in, one SCIM app for the directory —
          provisioning, offboarding, and group-driven roles.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>An <strong class="text-zinc-300">Okta administrator</strong>.</:item>
        <:item>An emisar <strong class="text-zinc-300">owner or admin</strong>.</:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso">Single sign-on</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Connecting an identity provider is a paid feature. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>emisar</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Start the connection.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open
              <a
                href={~p"/app/sso/new"}
                class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
              >Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</a>
              and choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Okta</strong>. Leave the page open: the
              callback URL on it is what Okta asks for next, and Okta's values come back here
              at the end.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-emisar-add-provider.webp"
              title="emisar · Add provider"
              caption="Picking the provider here tailors the rest of the form to it."
              alt="emisar's Add provider form with the Provider type select outlined in green and set to Okta."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Okta</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Create an OIDC web application.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In the Okta Admin Console go to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Applications → Applications → Create App Integration</strong>. Choose
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">OIDC - OpenID Connect</strong>
              as the sign-in method.
              Then choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Web Application</strong>
              as the application
              type. This creates the confidential client emisar needs. It authenticates with a
              client secret.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-oidc-create.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Create a new app integration"
              caption="Pick OIDC, and the Application type choices appear below it — emisar is a Web Application."
              alt="Okta's Create a new app integration dialog with OIDC - OpenID Connect selected among the sign-in methods and outlined in green together with its description, which reveals an Application type section below where Web Application is selected and likewise outlined rather than Single-Page or Native Application."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Okta</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Register emisar's callback as the only redirect.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Name the application, then set the sign-in controls explicitly:
            </p>
            <.docs_settings>
              <:setting label="Application name">
                Use <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar</code>, or another
                name your administrators will recognize.
              </:setting>
              <:setting label="Sign-in redirect URIs">
                Copy the callback from the Add provider page. It ends with
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>
                on your emisar host. Keep it as the only entry.
              </:setting>
              <:setting label="Allow wildcard *">
                Leave it off. A wildcard broadens which redirect destinations Okta will trust.
              </:setting>
              <:setting label="Grant type">
                Leave <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authorization Code</strong> selected.
              </:setting>
              <:setting label="Assignments">
                Choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Skip group assignment for now</strong>. Okta
                app assignment controls who may sign in; Emisar's
                <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/scim#role-mapping"}
                  class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
                >Role mapping</.link>
                separately controls which Emisar role a synced directory group grants.
              </:setting>
            </.docs_settings>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-oidc-settings.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · New Web App Integration"
              caption="One sign-in redirect URI, wildcard off, Authorization Code. Clear the sign-out box — emisar doesn't use it."
              alt="Okta's New Web App Integration form with the Grant type block and the Sign-in redirect URIs block each outlined in green: app integration name emisar, Authorization Code ticked under Core grants, https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback as the only sign-in redirect URI, the allow-wildcard box unchecked, and an empty sign-out redirect URI field."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Okta</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Copy the client credentials.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Saving lands you on the app's General tab with its
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client ID</strong>
              and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client secret</strong>. You need both, plus your <strong class="text-zinc-200">issuer</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-oidc-credentials.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Client Credentials"
              caption="Keep Client authentication on Client secret — emisar is a confidential client and replays that secret to your IdP."
              alt="The Okta app's General tab showing Client Credentials, with the Client ID row and the Client secrets table each outlined in green as the two values to copy: the Client ID with a copy button, Client authentication set to Client secret rather than public key, PKCE unchecked, and a masked client secret with its creation date and Active status."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            5
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>emisar</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Connect it in emisar.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Back on the connection you opened in step 1, paste the client ID and secret. For the
              issuer use your <strong class="text-zinc-200">org</strong>
              authorization server — that is your Okta org URL itself
              (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">https://your-org.okta.com</code>,
              with no <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/oauth2/…</code>
              path). Then tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled</strong>, save, and sign in.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-emisar-credentials.webp"
              title="emisar · Add provider"
              caption="Three values from Okta. The issuer is your org URL, not an authorization-server path."
              alt="emisar's OIDC connection fields with Issuer URL, Client ID and Client secret each outlined in green, and the Issuer URL placeholder showing https://YOUR-ORG.okta.com."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="The rest of the connection form">
        <p>
          This guide covers only the provider-specific fields.
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Single sign-on</.link>
          explains provisioning, default roles, runner access, and provider
          MFA settings.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Use the org authorization server, not a custom one.">
        <p>
          A <strong class="text-zinc-200">custom authorization server</strong>
          lets an Okta
          administrator rewrite the subject claim. Its tokens can use an email as
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
          instead of the Okta user
          ID. The organization server keeps
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
          equal to the Okta user ID.
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/scim"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">directory sync</.link>
          uses the same value, so login and provisioning identify one person.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="scim">Directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You need the connection's <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM base URL</strong>
        and <strong class="text-zinc-200">bearer token</strong>
        —
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim#enable"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >enabling directory sync</.link>
        shows where both come from. <.plan_note tier={:enterprise} />
      </p>
      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>An <strong class="text-zinc-300">Okta administrator</strong>.</:item>
        <:item>An emisar <strong class="text-zinc-300">owner or admin</strong>.</:item>
        <:item>
          The connection's <strong class="text-zinc-300">SCIM base URL</strong>
          and <strong class="text-zinc-300">bearer token</strong>, from the steps above.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="SCIM lives in its own Okta app">
        <p>
          Okta does not allow SCIM provisioning on an OIDC integration. Use separate integrations
          for sign-in and provisioning. This is an Okta limit, not an emisar limit. The
          provisioning application's sign-on does not need to work. It only carries the SCIM
          configuration.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Okta</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Add the SCIM 2.0 Header Auth app.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In the Okta Admin Console go to
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Applications → Applications → Browse App Catalog</strong>
              and search for <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM 2.0</strong>. Pick
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM 2.0 Test App (Header Auth)</strong>
              — Header Auth is the variant that sends the token the way emisar reads it — then <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add Integration</strong>. Name it something an
              administrator will recognise later, take the defaults on the Sign-On tab, and finish.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-scim-catalog.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Browse App Integration Catalog"
              caption="Four SCIM test apps share this name — take the 2.0 Header Auth one."
              alt="Okta's Browse App Integration Catalog with SCIM 2.0 Test App typed into the search box, showing a typeahead list of SCIM 2.0 Test App (Basic Auth), SCIM 2.0 Test App (Header Auth), SCIM 2.0 Test App (OAuth Bearer Token), SCIM 1.1 Test App (Header Auth), and SCIM 1.1 Test App (Basic Auth)."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Okta</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Point it at emisar and test the credentials.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              On the application's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>
              tab, choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure API Integration</strong>
              and enable API
              integration. Enter the SCIM base URL as <strong class="text-zinc-200">Base URL</strong>. Enter the bearer as <strong class="text-zinc-200">API Token</strong>. Paste the token exactly as emisar
              showed it. Okta sends it in the
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Authorization</code>
              header without a <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Bearer</code>
              keyword. The <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">ems-</code>
              prefix lets emisar accept it unambiguously. Select
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test API Credentials</strong>
              before saving.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-scim-verified.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Test API Credentials"
              caption="The green result confirms that Okta reached emisar with this URL and bearer."
              alt="Okta's SCIM API integration form after a successful live test, with a green verified-success message, the emisar SCIM base URL filled, the API Token masked, and Import Groups enabled."
            />
            <p class="mt-4">
              A failed test means the token or URL is wrong. Check for a truncated token and a base
              URL missing its
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/scim/v2</code>
              suffix. For a bad token, emisar returns <em>“The SCIM bearer token is missing,
              malformed, or not authorized.”</em>
              Okta shows this message. Rotate the token in
              emisar and paste the new one.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Okta</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Turn on the lifecycle operations.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Still under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>, open
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">To App</strong>
              → <strong class="text-zinc-200">Edit</strong>
              and set each operation as follows:
            </p>
            <.docs_settings>
              <:setting label="Create Users">
                <strong class="text-zinc-200">On.</strong> Assigned people can be provisioned.
              </:setting>
              <:setting label="Update User Attributes">
                <strong class="text-zinc-200">On.</strong> Profile changes reach Emisar.
              </:setting>
              <:setting label="Deactivate Users">
                <strong class="text-zinc-200">On.</strong> This makes offboarding automatic and
                also carries reactivation.
              </:setting>
              <:setting label="Sync Password">
                <strong class="text-zinc-200">Off.</strong> Emisar has no passwords to sync.
              </:setting>
            </.docs_settings>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-scim-to-app.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Provisioning to App"
              caption="Without these three, the integration authenticates but never pushes anything."
              alt="Okta's Provisioning to App settings in edit mode with Create Users, Update User Attributes and Deactivate Users all enabled, and Sync Password left off."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Okta</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Assign the people to sync.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Assignment is what actually provisions someone. On the
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Assignments</strong>
              tab, use <strong class="text-zinc-200">Assign</strong>
              → <strong class="text-zinc-200">Assign to People</strong>
              (or to a group). Each person you assign appears in emisar under
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Synced members</strong>
              at the connection's default role, and each one you unassign is deprovisioned there.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-scim-assignments.webp"
              title="Okta · Assignments"
              caption="This live assignment provisioned the certification user through the assigned Okta group."
              alt="The Assignments tab of the emisar directory sync application in Okta, with the Assign dropdown outlined in green and one assigned certification user listed below it."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            5
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Okta</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Push the groups you want to map access from.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Complete this step when Okta groups control roles or runner access. Groups travel on their
              own tab — <strong class="text-zinc-200">Push Groups</strong>, beside Assignments, not
              part of the To App settings you just turned on. Push a group here and it appears in
              emisar under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Synced groups</strong>, ready to map by its
              immutable directory-group identity.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-scim-push-groups.webp"
              title="Okta · Push Groups"
              caption="This group was pushed successfully and is Active. Assigning people does not push their groups."
              alt="The Push Groups tab of the emisar directory sync application in Okta, with the Push Groups action outlined in green and one certification group listed with Push Status Active."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Okta lines up on identity by default.">
        <p>
          Okta sends the user ID as SCIM <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>. ID tokens from the
          organization authorization server use the same value as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>. Sign-in and directory sync
          therefore identify one emisar member. Two
          things break that: a <strong class="text-zinc-200">custom</strong>
          authorization server, whose subject claim an admin can rewrite, and access tokens, whose
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
          is often an email. Use the org authorization server, and check one real user before you
          roll out.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <p class="mt-8 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next:</strong>
        configure
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim#role-mapping"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Role mapping</.link>
        or runner access for the synced groups that need them.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
